{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgENJHdgP76zfEE7nuNkc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both bugs come from the same mistake: `src/app.py` used `hidden`, which DynamoDB reserves as a keyword, as a plain attribute name instead of the `#alias` form DynamoDB requires. I've fixed it, and all 9 tests pass.\n\n- **Prod 500:** `hide()` writes with `SET hidden = ...`. DynamoDB rejects a reserved word written that way in an update with a `ValidationException`, which is exactly the error in `logs/prod-error.log`. So no story has ever been hidden through the API.\n- **Staging feed:** the manual hide with the AWS CLI did work, since it used `#h` for the name. The problem was in `feed()`, which asks for the `hidden` field in a plain list of fields to return. In that list DynamoDB doesn't raise an error for a reserved word; it just leaves the field out of every result. So `hidden` never reached the filter and every story passed. There's no cache and no wrong table. The crash was masking this: nothing had ever been hidden, so nobody saw that the filter didn't work. Fixing only the crash would have made Hide return success while the story stayed in the public feed.\n- **Not yet hit:** `archive()` checks `status = :published`, and `status` is also reserved. It isn't called by the API yet, but it would have failed the same way once the admin console started using it.\n\n**What I changed:**\n- **`src/app.py`:** every field name in every DynamoDB request now uses a `#alias`, via a small `_names()` helper. Method names and signatures are unchanged, so `api.py` doesn't need touching.\n- **`tests/test_feed.py`:** new tests cover hiding, a story hidden outside the library (like the CLI fix), hiding a slug that doesn't exist, and archiving. Against the old code, each one fails with the same error or wrong result seen on prod and staging.\n- **Keeping it from coming back:** the tests now run against a stricter fake table that rejects *any* field name written without an alias, reserved or not. The existing fake only knows part of DynamoDB's ~570 reserved words, so checking against that list would miss the next one.\n\nThe full write-up is in `ANSWER.md`. It also notes a separate problem I left alone: `feed()` reads the table in a single scan without paging, so once the table passes 1 MB the feed will quietly drop stories."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1514,"cache_read_input_tokens":32994,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1514},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cfcd3737-8af9-467e-aae4-f11393380a4c","uuid":"90c8d10a-5cdf-47c0-8e38-9f3d73bf1cff","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:37:02.397Z","request_id":"req_011CgENJHNZVXdeTBNC76Tvf"}
